# Test: show mtu exhaustive bypasses a poisoned path MTU
# Spec: spec-path-mtu-diagnostic -- user story 3, AC-11
# VALIDATES: the sender's route to 10.99.2.1 carries `mtu 1300`, so the kernel
#            refuses every larger honor-cache probe locally and `show mtu host`
#            answers 1300 `via local iface MTU`. `show mtu host ... exhaustive` opens
#            the socket in bypass mode, the router refuses at 1400, the payload
#            answers 1400, names the cached 1300 beside it, and a note says the
#            cache is stale.
# PREVENTS: an exhaustive run that still believes the kernel's estimate, and a stale
#           cache the operator is not told about.
#
# The path is three network namespaces joined by veth pairs, built by the
# compiled fixture `plugin/clamped-path` (internal/test/fixture, netlink): the
# daemon runs in the sender namespace, the router forwards with its far link at
# 1400, and the far namespace answers. Building them needs CAP_NET_ADMIN and
# the raw ICMP socket needs CAP_NET_RAW, so the test runs in the QEMU guest as
# root (`./le test qemu all-tests`) and skips in the unprivileged merge gate. The
# fixture removes the namespaces when the daemon exits, whatever its exit code.

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,net-raw

tmpfs=mtu-exhaustive.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external show-mtu-test {
		run "le test fixture plugin/show-mtu-exhaustive"
		encoder json
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=le test fixture plugin/clamped-path netns zemtuexhaustive$PORT route-mtu 1300 run ze start mtu-exhaustive.conf:timeout=60s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=OK: the poisoned 1300 held the honor-cache answer and exhaustive measured 1400 on the wire
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
